      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the least whole number of hours \\( n \\) greater than 4 hours, such that \\( n \\) is \"clock equivalent\" to its square number of hours. This means that \\( n \\) must satisfy the equation \\( n \\equiv n^2 \\pmod{12} \\).\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the congruence:\n\\[ n \\equiv n^2 \\pmod{12} \\]\nThis can be rearranged to:\n\\[ n^2 - n \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\]\nFactoring out \\( n \\), we get:\n\\[ n(n-1) \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\]\nThis equation tells us that \\( n(n-1) \\) must be divisible by 12. Since \\( n \\) and \\( n-1 \\) are consecutive integers, one of them must be divisible by 3, and one of them must be divisible by 4 (because among any two consecutive integers, one is even, and one is odd, and the product of an even number and an odd number is divisible by 2, and the product of three consecutive numbers is always divisible by 3 and 4).\n\nWe need to find the smallest \\( n > 4 \\) such that \\( n(n-1) \\) is divisible by 12. Let's check the values of \\( n \\) starting from 5:\n\nFor \\( n = 5 \\):\n\\[ 5(5-1) = 5 \\times 4 = 20 \\]\n20 is divisible by 4 but not by 3, so 5 is not a solution.\n\nFor \\( n = 6 \\):\n\\[ 6(6-1) = 6 \\times 5 = 30 \\]\n30 is divisible by both 3 and 4, so 6 is a solution.\n\nThus, the least whole number of hours that is greater than 4 hours and is \"clock equivalent\" to its square number of hours is:\n\\[ \\boxed{6} \\]",
