      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the smallest whole number greater than \\(4\\) that is \"clock equivalent\" to its square. This means we are looking for a number \\(n\\) such that the elapsed time \\(n\\) hours on a 12-hour clock is the same as the elapsed time \\(n^2\\) hours on the same clock.\n\nFirst, let's express the condition mathematically:\n\\[ n \\equiv n^2 \\pmod{12} \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ n^2 - n \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\]\n\\[ n(n-1) \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\]\nThis means that \\(n(n-1)\\) must be a multiple of \\(12\\). Since \\(n\\) and \\(n-1\\) are consecutive integers, one of them is always even, and one of them is always divisible by \\(3\\). Therefore, their product is always divisible by \\(2 \\times 2 \\times 3 = 12\\).\n\nWe need to find the smallest \\(n > 4\\) such that \\(n(n-1) \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12}\\). Let's check the values of \\(n\\) starting from \\(5\\):\n\n1. For \\(n = 5\\):\n\\[ 5 \\times 4 = 20 \\]\nSince \\(20 \\div 12 = 1\\) remainder \\(8\\), \\(20 \\not\\equiv 0 \\pmod{12}\\).\n\n2. For \\(n = 6\\):\n\\[ 6 \\times 5 = 30 \\]\nSince \\(30 \\div 12 = 2\\) remainder \\(6\\), \\(30 \\not\\equiv 0 \\pmod{12}\\).\n\n3. For \\(n = 7\\):\n\\[ 7 \\times 6 = 42 \\]\nSince \\(42 \\div 12 = 3\\) remainder \\(6\\), \\(42 \\not\\equiv 0 \\pmod{12}\\).\n\n4. For \\(n = 8\\):\n\\[ 8 \\times 7 = 56 \\]\nSince \\(56 \\div 12 = 4\\) remainder \\(8\\), \\(56 \\not\\equiv 0 \\pmod{12}\\).\n\n5. For \\(n = 9\\):\n\\[ 9 \\times 8 = 72 \\]\nSince \\(72 \\div 12 = 6\\) remainder \\(0\\), \\(72 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12}\\).\n\nThus, \\(n = 9\\) is the smallest whole number greater than \\(4\\) that is \"clock equivalent\" to its square. The answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]",
