    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "We want to find the least whole number of hours $n$ greater than $4$ such that $n$ is \"clock equivalent'' to $n^2$. This means that $n$ and $n^2$ must have the same remainder when divided by $12$.\n\nSince $n$ must be greater than $4$, the possible values for $n$ are $5, 6, 7, 8, 9,$ and $10$.\n\nWe can then check each of these values to see if they are \"clock equivalent\" to their square:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n5^2 &= 25 \\\\\n6^2 &= 36 \\\\\n7^2 &= 49 \\\\\n8^2 &= 64 \\\\\n9^2 &= 81 \\\\\n10^2 &= 100\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nNow, we find the remainders when each of these values is divided by $12$:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n5^2 &\\equiv 1 \\pmod{12} \\\\\n6^2 &\\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\\\\n7^2 &\\equiv 1 \\pmod{12} \\\\\n8^2 &\\equiv 4 \\pmod{12} \\\\\n9^2 &\\equiv 9 \\pmod{12} \\\\\n10^2 &\\equiv 4 \\pmod{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nNone of these values are $0$ modulo $12$, so none of them are \"clock equivalent'' to $n^2$. However, we do notice that $5$ and $7$ are \"clock equivalent'' to themselves, and $6$ and $8$ also have the same remainder of $0$. Thus, the least whole number of hours that is greater than $4$ and \"clock equivalent'' to its square number of hours is $\\boxed{6}$.The answer is: 6",
